/** Culture and industry essays. Dates absolute. No new unverified statistics. */
module.exports = {
  "oil-and-chrome": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "SOPHIE died in Athens on 30 January 2021. The records, from BIPP to OIL OF EVERY PEARL'S UN-INSIDES, were already finished.",
    paras: [
      `SOPHIE died in Athens on 30 January 2021, after a fall. The news moved the way news moves now: a photograph, a statement, a rush of people who had used the word hyperpop as a sticker discovering, too late, that a sticker is not a body of work. The body of work was already there. <em>PRODUCT</em> in 2015, on Numbers, with “BIPP” as the track that sounded like a plastic object learning to sing. <em>OIL OF EVERY PEARL’S UN-INSIDES</em> in 2018, with “It’s Okay to Cry” and “Immaterial.” A collaboration history with PC Music and with Charli XCX that gave a lot of listeners their first clean hit of the sound and their easiest excuse not to learn the rest.`,
      `What the records actually did, underneath the tribute cycle, was separate sound design from the assumption that electronic music should imitate acoustic bodies. The kicks are too shiny. The bass distorts in a way that feels like a material failing on purpose, the way latex fails if you treat it as skin. Voices slide between registers without asking the listener to decide on a gender story first; the music has already decided that timbre is an instrument. “Immaterial” says the philosophical part out loud, and it would be a lesser song if it were only philosophy. It is a pop song. The hook is humiliatingly catchy. That combination — a club track you can argue about and a melody you cannot shake — is the part imitators kept splitting. They took the metallic snare or they took the manifesto. SOPHIE kept them in the same bar.`,
      `“BIPP” is the earlier, ruder version of the idea. It promises ecstasy in the lyric and then builds a rhythm that feels like an object being squeezed. The joke and the wound arrive together, which is why copying the palette never quite worked. You can buy the same distortion. You cannot buy the timing of the joke. By the time the copyists had festival slots, the original records still sounded like the only finished versions, because they were finished. They were not sketches toward a trend. <em>OIL OF EVERY PEARL’S UN-INSIDES</em> is an album with a shape: the ballad at the start, the chaos in the middle, “Immaterial” as a thesis you can dance to, the long unspooling of “Whole New World / Pretend World.” Albums were not supposed to be the point of this sound. She made one anyway, and it holds.`,
      `Athens, as the place of death, got turned into a symbol by people who needed a symbol. It is a city, and it is where a terrible accident happened. The records were made elsewhere, in a career that ran through Glasgow — SOPHIE was from Glasgow — and through London’s PC Music orbit and through a life that became more public as the music did. A. G. Cook’s circle got a paragraph in every obituary. Charli got a paragraph. Both paragraphs are true and secondary. The primary text is the discography, which you can play without a timeline. Playing it is the part of mourning that does not require anyone to perform grief in public. “It’s Okay to Cry” is, among other things, a set of instructions that are better than the ones a comments section will give you. The song is gentle and it is not vague.`,
      `The years since have been a lesson in how quickly a radical sound becomes a preset. Hyperpop, as a playlist genre, sanded the specific records into a loudness contest and a visual code. SOPHIE’s music is loud, and there is a visual world around it, but the loudness is arranged. There are dynamics. There is air in “Is It Cold in the Water?” and there is none in “Ponyboy,” and the difference is the composition. A preset has no difference. It has a setting. The respectful way to be influenced is to accept that the setting will not save you, and to write a song anyway. A lot of the influence has been the setting.`,
      `This page used to end by telling the reader to leave the oil on and not to make her a saint. The second instruction is still the right critical caution: sainthood is another way of not listening, because saints are finished and these records are arguments. The first instruction can be simpler. The album is called <em>OIL OF EVERY PEARL’S UN-INSIDES</em>. It came out in 2018. It is not a metaphor for a scene. It is a tracklist. Athens is where she died, on the last day of January 2021. The work she had already finished is the part that does not depend on how the timeline behaved that week.`
    ]
  },
  "the-file-is-the-record": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "In 2021 the pressing plants were backed up and Bandcamp Friday waived its cut. The copy you could pay for was a file.",
    paras: [
      `By September 2021, a person who wanted a new independent record on vinyl was often buying a promise. Pressing plants, jammed by a pandemic-era boom and by major-label represses of catalogues that did not need another colored variant, were quoting waits measured in many months. Cassette duplicators had the same kind of queue at a smaller scale. The object existed as a pre-order page and a shipping date that slipped. The music existed as a file. Pretending the file was a compromise and the vinyl was the real culture got the sequence backwards. You cannot keep what you cannot hear. The file was how you heard it.`,
      `Bandcamp Friday, which the company began in March 2020, waived the platform’s revenue share on the first Friday of the month and sent that share to the artists. It ran, on and off, long after the first lockdown. It was not a lifestyle. It was a till. One morning a month, the checkout button pointed more honestly at the person who made the record. Listeners who were waiting on a plant could pay for the music on that morning and live with the WAV. That is not a degraded version of ownership. It is ownership of the part that plays. The 12-inch, when it arrived, was a second copy with a different job: a shelf, a ritual, a mix you do with your hands. Both copies can be real. The moralism that says only the heavy one counts was a luxury opinion in a year when the heavy one was stuck in a queue.`,
      `Crate culture’s insistence on the object has a good root and a bad branch. The good root is that music is work and work should be paid, and that a physical edition forces a label to decide a record is finished. The bad branch is the belief that a night only happened if you can grade the sleeve. Bedrooms had already disproved that. A cheap upload could move a room if the room was ready. PinkPantheress’s <em>To Hell With It</em>, released the month after this page first ran, in October 2021, made the proof public at pop scale: short songs, garage drums, a file that was the record. The plant was irrelevant to whether the songs were finished. The plant is relevant to whether a particular edition exists. Those are different questions, and the discourse kept welding them together.`,
      `The checkout button that does not go to the artist is the actual opponent. Streaming’s problem is not that it is a file. Files are fine. The problem is a payment system that treats the file as a reason to pay almost no one, and a discovery system that treats your attention as the product. A download you bought on a Friday when the share was waived is a file with a different politics. It sits on a drive you control. It plays if the company changes its mind. It can be copied into the car. None of that requires a sermon about the superiority of the past. The past, for independent music, included a lot of tapes that were also files in a slower form: a sequence of audio, a hand-written label, a postal service.`,
      `Club Copy’s shop lives in this argument. Digital editions are the ones that can be delivered the day the page goes up. Cassettes and other physical runs are the ones that can sell out, as <em>You Are (Love)</em> did. “Music we want to keep,” the line on the about page, does not specify the molecule. Keeping is a decision to pay and to have the thing again tomorrow. A label that ships nothing unless you choose it, which is the club model described on the site, is making the same distinction Bandcamp Friday made visible: choice, then a file or an object, then a person who got the money. The romance of the plant is optional. The payment is not.`,
      `The September 2021 version of this essay told diggers they could sit the year out if they only counted a night they could grade. That was ruder than it needed to be and directionally right. Grading is a hobby. Hearing the record is the culture. If the 12-inch is a tracking number, you still have the copy you paid for, and the copy is what the artist finished. When the box finally comes, it will be a pleasure of a different kind. It will not retroactively make the file a sketch. The file was the record. The vinyl is the souvenir that, in a good year, catches up.`
    ]
  },
  "the-algorithm-is-not-a-room": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "A recommendation is a corridor. A room has a door, a night, and a person who decided the next record.",
    paras: [
      `A recommendation engine can build you a corridor that feels like taste. It has your plays, your skips, the plays of people who look like you in a spreadsheet. Spotify Wrapped, once a year, turns that corridor into a brochure and hands it back as a personality. Resident Advisor can tell you which night is happening and still be a brochure of a different kind: listings, not the night. None of these objects is a room. A room has a door, a capacity, a person who brought the records, and a set of bodies who will hear the same thing at the same time whether or not a system thinks they should.`,
      `The confusion is understandable, because the corridor got very good. By 2022, when this page first tried to say so, the feed was often louder than the PA in the sense that more people encountered the music as a clip than as a set. A clip is a real way to hear a record. It is a bad way to understand a night. The night has a duration and a social risk. You might not get in. You might hate the third hour. You might have the best conversation of your year with someone whose name you do not keep. A For You page cannot fake those outcomes. It can fake the soundtrack, and the fake is flattering, which is why it works as a product.`,
      `This is not an argument for leaving the phone at home, a piece of advice that has hardened into its own brochure. People find the address in a thread. They screenshot the flyer. They record a clip that, later, is how someone else decides to buy a record. Fine. The lie is the one that says the app is the night, or that a year of listening can be summarized by a graphic you did not sit for. Wrapped is a yearbook. Yearbooks are fun and they are not the school. The school, in this metaphor, is the boring part: choosing a record on purpose, playing it all the way through, finding out that your alleged taste was a handful of skips and one album you actually live with.`,
      `Dance music keeps producing examples that the corridor then takes credit for. PinkPantheress’s songs moved through short video and were still songs, written and sung, with garage drums that came from a tradition a recommendation might later classify. Eliza Rose and Interplanetary Criminal’s “B.O.T.A.” moved through DJs and then through the chart. The chart is not an algorithm, exactly. It is a count. The count came after the rooms. When a service’s year-end graphic includes the song, the graphic is a mirror held up late. It is not the reason the chant existed. Remembering the order — room, then count, then brochure — is the whole media literacy this subject requires.`,
      `Labels live in the corridor and in the room at once. A Bandcamp page is a corridor with a till, which is better than a corridor without one. A club night is a room. Club Copy, on the evidence of its own site, is mostly a till and a zine: releases with numbers, a mixes page, a shop. It is not a room with a capacity. Pretending a web shop is a club is the same category error as pretending a playlist is a night. The honest version is smaller and more useful. We sell records and we write about music. Sometimes the music was made for rooms we do not run. The listener’s job is to notice which object they are holding.`,
      `If you only heard something because a machine guessed your demographic, the next step is not a purge. It is a second listen you chose. Choice is the difference between a corridor and a door. You can walk a corridor forever and feel accompanied. A door commits you to one night, one sequence, one set of people who also chose, or stumbled in, and had to stay with the consequences. The past of this music — pirate radio, flyers, shops, word of mouth — was a set of doors with bad signage. The signage is better now. The doors are still the part that counts.`
    ]
  },
  "no-prompt-can-touch": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "A model can imitate a finished track. It cannot supply the months a person disappeared into before the bridge existed.",
    paras: [
      `The scare with generative music was never that a machine can make a sound. Synthesizers are machines. Samplers are machines. A laptop is a machine. The scare is that a model can make something that sounds finished, quickly, without anyone having lived with the unfinished version. Finished and true start sharing a jacket. Listeners who learned to trust surface — a clean vocal, a plausible drop, a lyric that scans — lose a test they did not know they were using. The test was: did a person stay in the room long enough for the room to change the song?`,
      `On 8 March 2024 this page used Nihil Young’s <em>Compass</em> as the counter-example at hand: a record built, in his account of it, from things that had actually happened, a marriage, a child, a father’s death. The point was not that autobiography is the only legitimate subject. Instrumental records are legitimate, and plenty of great songs are inventions. The point was cost. A bridge that exists because of what it cost the writer is different from a bridge that exists because the statistical next line was the bridge. You cannot hear the cost with certainty. You can hear, over time, the difference between a body of work that keeps faith with a life and a stream of tracks that keep faith with a prompt.`,
      `Hayao Miyazaki, shown an early artificial-intelligence animation demo, called it an insult to life itself. The line is harsh and it has been quoted into the ground, usually without the specific demo that provoked it. It still aims at the right hole. A machine can wear the shape of a feeling. Shape is what models are for: the distribution of shapes people have already made. Life, in Miyazaki’s insult, means the part that is not a distribution — the ugly particular, the grief that does not generalize, the joke that is only funny if you were there. Music has its own version. A song can be made of common parts and still contain one decision that only makes sense because of a private fact. The private fact may never be disclosed. The decision remains.`,
      `Every label that sits on records, including this one, has a version of the wait. A sketch that was not a song yet. A night that refused to be efficient. The wait is easy to romanticize and easy to fake. Romanticizing it produces the myth of the suffering artist, which has covered a lot of ordinary bad behavior. Faking it produces liner notes about process that the music does not earn. The practical version of the wait is less glamorous. You play the loop until you are sick of it. You notice the bar that is lying. You throw out a chorus you were proud of yesterday. None of that is available to a prompt, because a prompt does not get sick of anything. It does not have a yesterday.`,
      `There is a use of these tools that does not trigger the insult. A mixer uses a machine. A drummer uses a machine when the drum is electronic. The line is whether the person can still tell the truth about what they rejected. If you can say “the model offered this and I threw it out because it was the average of the song,” you are producing. If you cannot tell the average from the song, you are curating a slot. Curation is a real job and it is not the same job. Club Copy’s pages keep saying, in different words, that records are finished by people: Forsberg’s “finished when they leave,” the production credit on <em>j'adore</em>, Riscape’s split between a writer’s name and a producer’s name. Those sentences are claims about persons. They are testable only by the records, but they are the right claims to make in a year when the alternative is a finished-sounding file with no one willing to own the ugly version.`,
      `The part no prompt can touch is small and easy to overstate. It is not “soul,” a word that has excused too much. It is accountability over time. A person who puts their name on a record will have to hear it in a year, in a room, next to the last one they signed. A model does not hear. Leaving the rejected take in the folder, which this desk does as a matter of craft and not of purity, is a way of keeping the accountability visible to yourself. The folder is not a moral stage. It is how you prove, later, that the finished version was a choice.`
    ]
  },
  "the-group-chat-is-the-door": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "Resident Advisor lists the night and Dice sells the ticket. The address still moves the way it moved in 1998: person to person.",
    paras: [
      `Resident Advisor is a magazine and a listings service. Dice is a ticket company. Both are useful and neither is the start of the night. The night starts when a specific person tells a specific other person where to be, and accepts the risk of having told them. In 1998 that was three missed calls and a phone that died on the way. In the mid-2000s it was a mass text with a back door and a name to give. In the years since it has been a thread with a pin. The tool changed. The politics did not: who has the address, who has the money, who will leave if the door is hostile, who is trusted not to bring the wrong crowd.`,
      `Calling this a discovery about social media misses the history. Dance music has always been semi-private at the point of entry, including when it was most public. A pirate station could tell you the record and not the warehouse. A flyer could tell you a phone number and the phone number could tell you a street. The secrecy was sometimes safety, sometimes snobbery, sometimes both. Women organizing nights have done the practical version forever, because the practical version is how you get your friends home. A scene that only remembers the promoter’s name on the flyer has forgotten the person who actually decided the list.`,
      `The listings site became a brochure when the night turned into content. You can browse a city you are not in and feel briefly that you belong to its weekend. Belonging, in that mode, is a feeling sold by a calendar. It is not nothing — people do find their first night that way, and a good listing is a public service — but it is not the same as being told, by someone who has decided you will not ruin it, that the door is on the left. The ticket app became a receipt. Receipts are good. They are how you prove you paid. They do not know whether the room was any good, and they cannot introduce you to the person you came to stand next to.`,
      `There is a snobbery available on the other side, and this magazine has indulged it: if you are not in the thread, you arrive as a tourist. Sometimes that is true. Often it is a way for people who got the address early to feel like a class. A healthy night has a door for the stranger. The stranger’s path might be the listing, the receipt, the friend of a friend. The path is not the problem. The problem is a culture that pretends the path is the culture, and a counter-culture that pretends the path is contamination. Both stories leave out the music. The music is why any of the paths are worth the trouble.`,
      `What the thread is good at, and what a listing is bad at, is the last-hour correction. The club has moved. The guest list is under a different name. Someone needs a charger and someone else is already in the car. That correction is the actual social life of a scene, and it is invisible to anyone writing the history from flyers alone. Historians of this music should treat the missed call and the pin as documents, even when the documents are gone. The flyer is what was willing to be public. The message is what got people in. Archives that only keep the public part will overrate promoters and underrate friends.`,
      `Are we going: the question is older than the app that currently carries it. It is the whole ethics of a night in one line. You answer it with your time and your money and your body, not with a reaction. If the messages go quiet, the night has started without you, or it has been cancelled, and you will find out which by whether you can hear your own house. Both outcomes are a kind of evening. Only one of them needs an address. The address is a responsibility. Whoever has it is running more of the culture than the logo on the listing.`
    ]
  },
  "the-after-is-the-point": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "The photograph is the brochure. The night that matters starts when someone plays a record and nobody votes on it.",
    paras: [
      `Every public picture of a night is a brochure with good lighting. A strobe, someone else’s shoulder, a caption that places the photographer closer to the booth than they were. The brochure is not a lie about the fact that people danced. It is a lie about proportion. It makes the peak look like the whole. Anyone who has stayed past the peak knows the night’s actual shape: a queue, a room, a bathroom where you learn whether the night is going to be kind, a walk, and then a smaller room where someone puts on music without asking the group what it wants.`,
      `The smaller room is the one this music keeps trying to talk about and keeps turning into content. Afters, in British club language, are not a brand. They are what happens when the licensed hour ends and the people who are not done yet follow each other somewhere. The room is smaller than the club; that is not the point. The point is that the DJ, if there is one, is now a person you can see choosing, and the choice is social. A bad choice empties the kitchen. A good one makes the hour feel like the reason you came out, which is an awkward thing to admit about a night you have already described as the club.`,
      `We spent a decade letting the queue stand in for the culture. The queue photographs well. It has a narrative of exclusion and desire, and magazines, including this one, know how to write it. The kitchen photographs badly. Someone is sitting on the floor. The speaker is too small. The track would be laughed out of a peak-time set because it is too slow, or too vocal, or too obviously a song. Forsberg’s <em>j'adore</em>, two tracks released 10 August 2026, is the kind of record that lives more honestly in that hour than in a tent. That is not a claim about a specific afters. It is a claim about scale. A four-minute house song with a named producer fits a room where people are talking. It does not need a drop to justify itself. Neither does Sade’s <em>Love Deluxe</em>, which this magazine has written about as apartment music for the same reason. Different decades, same job: hold a room that is no longer performing.`,
      `The critical mistake is to treat the afters as the authentic night and the club as the sellout. Clubs are where most people hear loud music on a proper system, and a proper system is not a moral failure. The mistake runs the other way in the brochures: treating the club as the whole and the rest as residue. Residue is where you find out whether you still like the people you came with. That information is not aesthetic. It is the social product of the evening, and the music is what gives it a tempo. Somebody has to choose the tempo. The moment nobody takes a vote is the moment the night becomes a work of taste rather than a negotiation. Taste can be wrong. Negotiation is how you end up with a playlist named after the group.`,
      `Labels that only sound like Friday have missed the buyer they say they want. The person who stays is the person who owns the record later, because they heard it when the night had stopped advertising itself. Club Copy’s catalogue, read uncharitably, is full of Friday adjectives: house, jungle, garage. Read by the clock, several of the records are built at song length, with titles about love and waiting and weather. Those are after-midnight subjects even when the drums are functional. A label does not have to throw the afters to make records for it. It has to notice that the peak is a poor editor. The peak likes everything. The smaller room is picky.`,
      `The photograph will keep winning the public argument, because it circulates and the kitchen does not. Fine. The archive can say the proportion correctly. A night is a sequence. The part with the best lighting is the part that least needs a writer. The part where someone puts a record on, and the room either stays or starts looking for a coat, is the part that tells you what the music was for.`
    ]
  },
  "the-object-came-back": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "Luminate's first-half-of-2026 figures said physical revenue was up 26 percent. The object came back. The reason to buy it did not change.",
    paras: [
      `In the first half of 2026, Luminate’s figures — the ones that landed in public discussion by the time this page ran in September — put physical music revenue up 26 percent. Vinyl was climbing. CDs were back in the conversation, including the awkward fact, also from that reporting, that a large share of millennial CD buyers did not own a player. The number is the kind of number the industry loves, because it sounds like a plot twist. The plot twist is smaller when you remember why people buy objects. They buy them so the night has a spine. A file can be the record, as this magazine has argued. An object is a decision you can hold while you are not listening, which is most of the time.`,
      `The player-less CD buyer is not a paradox if you stop defining ownership as playback. Playback is one job. The other jobs are older: a gift, a shelf that shows a guest who you are when you are not explaining yourself, a tour edition you bought because you were in the room, a disc that survives a service shutting down. Some of those jobs are vanity. Vanity is allowed to fund a pressing plant. The plant does not know whether you own a tray to put the disc in. It knows you paid. The snobbery that insists a format only counts if you use it in the canonical way is the same snobbery that, in 2021, could not see a WAV as a real copy. Use is various. Payment is the consistent part.`,
      `What did change, and what the percentage blurs, is where the object is bought. Independent shops spent the 2010s rebuilding a reason to exist after the first digital collapse, then spent the early 2020s watching vinyl become a big-box product with exclusive colorways. A Target variant is not the enemy of music. It is a different door, with a different cut of the money and a different chance that the buyer will ever see a record store. The comeback is real and the raid is real. Both can be true in the same half-year. A shop that had a dead section in 2021 and a waitlist for dance 12-inches later is a local fact this page has reported in general terms; it is not a statistic to stretch. The visible pattern is enough: the object returned, and the corporations that do not otherwise run scenes were happy to sell it.`,
      `Cassette plants laughing, then not laughing, at a run of a couple of hundred is the small-label version of the same story. A tape is a bad format in several technical ways and a good one in a single social way. It is cheap enough to edition, physical enough to sell out, awkward enough that owning it means you meant to. Club Copy’s first cassette, <em>You Are (Love)</em>, sold out. That fact is a data point of one. It matches the wider return of the object without proving anything about Luminate’s 26 percent. One tape is a decision a few people made. The percentage is a decision a market made. Writers should not use one to decorate the other.`,
      `Streaming pays, poorly, for the play. The object pays, once, for the keeping. A sane listener uses both and refuses the sermon that says one of them is a personality. The sermon is having a moment because the numbers moved. Numbers move for reasons that include Taylor Swift variants and Record Store Day and the simple fact that a generation that grew up on files got curious about weight. Curiosity is a good reason to buy a record and a bad reason to announce the end of the file. You will still send your friends links. You will still, if you are this label’s customer, download a WAV after checkout. The sleeve on the kitchen table is an extra copy with a job the WAV does not want. Let it do that job. Do not make it a manifesto.`,
      `The first-half figures will be replaced by another year’s figures, and someone will say the object left again or came back harder. The durable part is the spine. A night you paid for twice — once as a ticket or a drink, once as a disc you can find without logging in — is a night with a backbone. The disc can be a CD you cannot play. The backbone, then, is the memory and the money, which is less romantic and more accurate. Buy the format you will actually live with. Pay the people who made it, at the door that gets them the money. The percentage can stay in Luminate’s report, where it is doing its job.`
    ]
  },
  "she-kept-the-flyer": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "The flyer was the first pressing of the night: cheap paper, a date, and a record of a room that did not issue one.",
    paras: [
      `Record culture learned to archive itself with grading, and so it trusts the objects that can be graded. A 12-inch has a matrix number. A flyer has a crease. The crease is closer to the truth of a night than the matrix is, because the night was a few hours in a room and the paper was what the room gave you to carry out. Fridge, wallet, the back of a book, a stub that only says you paid. Ink that wants to leave. This is an unimpressive archive and it is the one most people actually have.`,
      `The flyer was the first pressing. Before the recording, if there even was a recording, there was a promise: a date, a lineup, a graphic made too fast, a font someone will later call iconic because it survived. You took it because the night might not be repeated and because someone had written on the back. Diggers who talk first pressings and never talk paper have confused the commodity with the event. The commodity matters. The event is why there was a commodity. Forty-odd dollars for a disco 12-inch is a market. The xerox that smells like the floor is a document of a different market, the one where you paid with your evening.`,
      `Paper ephemera has a scholarship, and club music barely uses it. Theatre keeps playbills. Sport keeps tickets. Dance music, suspicious of institutions and bad at funding libraries, keeps what individual packrats kept. The packrats are doing the work of a museum without the climate control. When a shop or a fashion brand tries to sell a decade back to the people who lived it, the flyer on the fridge is the rebuttal. You do not need the reissue. You were there, and the proof is embarrassing and specific: a name spelled wrong, a guest who cancelled, a door time that was a lie. Official history cleans those up. The cleaning is how scenes become brands.`,
      `None of this means you should laminate the handbill. Lamination is another kind of grading. It says the paper has graduated from use to relic. Relics do not have to be protected from their own curl. The curl is evidence of a fridge and of summers. An archive that wants everything flat wants everything neutral. Neutral is not what a night was. If you are the person who keeps these things, you are allowed to keep them in a box without becoming a curator. You are also allowed to throw them out. A culture that cannot tell the difference between a person and a librarian is a culture that has replaced memory with inventory.`,
      `There is a musical point, not only a sentimental one. Flyers are how you reconstruct a repertoire. Who played, in what order of billing, in what room, next to what other name. Researchers of disco, reggae, and rave who do the work properly use paper because the records do not list the nights. A white label does not say where it was first played. The flyer might. Losing the paper means losing the map of how songs moved through cities. Discogs will not save you. Discogs knows editions. It does not know that a dubplate was heard by two hundred people on a Thursday and then never heard in that form again.`,
      `So the fridge is a stacks system. It is disorganized and it is local and it outperforms a feed at one task: holding a date still. The takeout menu beside the flyer is part of the same evening’s evidence, if you want to be honest about how nights end. Honest archives include the ordinary. The boys grading vinyl can keep their scale. The paper that cannot be graded is doing a job the scale was not built for. Leave it curled. Know which night it was. That is the whole collection.`
    ]
  },
  "the-camera-got-a-landlord": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "On 15 January 2025 DICE sold Boiler Room to Superstruct. The stream had a landlord. The night never had.",
    paras: [
      `On 15 January 2025, DICE sold Boiler Room to Superstruct Entertainment. Superstruct is the live-music group KKR bought, in a deal reported at about $1.4 billion, in the summer of 2024. The sale price of Boiler Room itself was not disclosed. DICE kept the ticketing relationship in the framing of the deal; Superstruct kept the brand. Blaise Bellville’s project — a camera in a room, a DJ, a comment section that made a basement feel like a passport — now reported to a festival conglomerate owned by private equity. That is a change of letterhead. It is also the logical end of a fifteen-year bet that a rectangle could stand in for a door.`,
      `The bet was not stupid. Boiler Room solved a real problem. Most people who care about dance music do not live in the city where a given set is happening, and most clubs will not pay to fly them there. A fixed camera and a decent sound feed let a teenager in another country study a DJ’s hands. At its best, the form was a lesson: track selection in real time, the boredom between peaks, the way a room leans. At its worst, it was a stage play about underground culture, with the crowd cast as extras and the DJ as a brand that could be clipped. Both versions traveled. The company’s own figures, repeated in coverage of the sale, talked about a billion views and well over a hundred markets. Reach is not a moral category. It is the reason a conglomerate wanted the name.`,
      `What the underground gave up, in exchange for the reach, was the ability to pretend the camera was neutral. A camera changes the set. DJs play records they are willing to have archived. Crowds perform having a good time, which is not the same as having one. Promoters book lineups that will travel as video. None of this is unique to one company — every phone in the room was already doing a cruder version — but Boiler Room industrialized it and gave it a house style. The house style became, for a decade, the global image of “a DJ in a small room,” even when the room was a sponsored set built for the lens. When the style was sold, people who had used it as their doorway felt the doorway change owners. The feeling is legitimate. The doorway was never theirs. It was a media company.`,
      `KKR’s ownership of Superstruct is the part that makes the story about capital rather than about a founder cashing out, though it is also about a founder cashing out. Private equity buys predictable cash flows and brands that can be slotted into a portfolio of festivals and venues. A livestream brand fits if the livestream is a funnel into live events and sponsorships. The music, in that model, is the content that keeps the brand warm. DJs who played Boiler Room for the love of a format now have to decide whether the format’s new landlord is a landlord they will work for. Some will say yes because the audience is there. Some will say no because the audience was never the same thing as the owner. Both decisions are adult. The childish one is shock that a media company could be sold.`,
      `The night, as opposed to the stream, was unaffected in the only way that matters and affected in the way that doesn’t. Unaffected: a good set in a real room on 16 January 2025 did not need the brand. Affected: the imaginary global room, the one where everyone was supposedly together because they were watching the same feed, had its deed transferred. Imaginary rooms are part of how this culture reproduces. Losing one to a balance sheet is worth writing down. It is not worth confusing with the loss of an actual club. Actual clubs close for rent, licenses, and noise complaints. Those are different landlords.`,
      `Put on a set you remember for the records, not for the angle of the shot. If you cannot separate them, the camera did its job too well. The rectangle has a new owner. The selection, if it was any good, belongs to the person who made it and to anyone who learned a transition from it. That is a smaller commons than the comment section promised. It is the one that was real.`
    ]
  },
  "the-door-still-says-no": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "In the 2026 DJ Mag poll Berghain placed 21st. A ticket, the club made clear, is not an admission.",
    paras: [
      `Berghain, in the 2026 DJ Mag Top 100 Clubs poll, placed twenty-first. The top of the list went to a new Ibiza hyperclub with a short history and a long capacity. Berghain was reported to have slid several places and to be the only Berlin club left on the list. A poll of that kind measures a blur of reputation, votes, size, and the willingness of a venue to be legible to a magazine. Berghain has spent two decades being selectively illegible. Position 21 is a brochure result. It does not change the queue on Rüdersdorfer Straße.`,
      `The more interesting 2026 story was the ticket page. Coverage through the year, including in Groove and Faze, described official tickets for selected nights in Säule and Panorama Bar, among them the house-leaning Finest Friday series. A cap of four tickets. Klubnacht — the main, long, weekend night — staying door-only. And the clause that keeps the whole experiment from meaning what a casual reader will think it means: the door can refuse you, cancel the ticket, and refund you. You bought a place in a line. You did not buy an identity. You did not buy the right to be inside.`,
      `For readers who have never stood in that line, the system needs a plain description. Berghain’s door is famous because it is a person, or a small number of people, making a decision you cannot appeal. The decision has been mythologized into a theory of cool, parodied, and experienced as humiliation or relief. It is also a practical instrument for a club that wants a room to behave a certain way over many hours: a mix of people who will stay, dance, and not treat the building as a safari. Ticketing the smaller floors and not the main night is an attempt to take money and planning pressure off some of the queue without handing the main room to anyone who can pay. Whether the attempt works is a question for the people in the room, not for a poll.`,
      `The poll and the ticket page pull in opposite directions, and the opposition is the point. DJ Mag rewards legibility and scale. A five-thousand-capacity club that wants to be photographed will beat a club that confiscates phones, on the metrics a public vote can see. Berghain’s partial ticketing rewards neither openness nor purity. It rewards a managed exception. Four tickets, a refund if the door says no, the Saturday-into-Monday night left alone. That is a conservative policy wearing the costume of a modernization. Conservative is not an insult here. A room that has lasted by saying no should be expected to modernize without giving the no away.`,
      `There is a wider industry mood in the background. Clubs that spent the 2010s as pilgrimage sites discovered, after the pandemic, that pilgrimage is a volatile business model. Advance tickets smooth the volatility. They also change the crowd, because a crowd that planned is different from a crowd that showed up. Berghain’s refund clause is an attempt to keep the second crowd’s logic inside the first crowd’s paperwork. It is fiddly and it is honest. Honest is more than most venues manage when they discover that their door policy has a resale value.`,
      `Position 21 will be a different number in another year’s poll. The clause is the part to keep. A ticket is a financial instrument. Admission is a social decision. Clubs that collapse the two will get a smoother spreadsheet and a worse room. Clubs that pretend the spreadsheet is sin will get a myth and a cash-flow problem. The 2026 compromise, at one building in Berlin, was to sell a little of the line and keep the right to undo the sale. The list is a brochure. The door is the product.`
    ]
  },
  "drill-lyrics-on-trial": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "In September 2026 the Met defended drill lyrics as evidence. A report counted 252 defendants in three years.",
    paras: [
      `A verse is a made thing. It can describe a crime, invent one, boast about one, quote a film about one, or do all of those in a couplet built to sound harder than the writer’s week. In September 2026 the Metropolitan Police defended the use of drill videos and lyrics as evidence in murder trials, after the campaign group Art Not Evidence asked for the law to change. A report cited in that fight found lyrics used against 252 defendants over three years. That is not an anecdote. It is a method.`,
      `The police argument, as detectives have put it in these cases, is that young people sometimes put real information about real violence into songs, because status in the scene rewards it, and that investigators would be negligent to ignore what is said in public. A mother whose son was killed, and whose case involved drill lyrics, has said the same from the other side of grief: if it is acceptable to write about a death, it is acceptable to use the writing to try to get justice. That position deserves to be stated without a sneer. Grief is not a panel discussion. Anyone writing about this from a magazine desk should feel how cheap a free-speech slogan sounds next to a parent.`,
      `Art Not Evidence’s argument is the one the law has to answer anyway. Courts have treated the aesthetics of a genre — a mask, a slang term, a beat, a reference to a postcode — as if they were admissions. Drill’s conventions include first person, named enemies, and a theatrical relationship to violence that the music itself often marks as performance and that a jury may not know how to mark. The scrutiny falls overwhelmingly on young Black men. Artists edit themselves because a bar might be printed in a bundle and read flat. Self-editing of that kind is a cultural effect you can predict without a theory degree. If the state builds a pipeline from a verse to a charge, the verse will get vaguer, not because the city got safer, but because the artists noticed the pipeline.`,
      `The hard case is the one both sides would prefer not to be the only case. Sometimes a lyric does contain a fact that is also evidence: a detail that was not public, a weapon, a time. A rule that said “never admit a lyric” would protect boasts and also protect a confession that happened to rhyme. A rule that says “always admit a lyric” treats a genre as a diary. The adult position is the annoying one. Lyrics should clear a high bar before a jury hears them. The bar should include context about how the genre speaks, expert evidence that is not just a police officer’s familiarity, and a distinction between a conventional boast and a specific claim. A bill before Parliament, in the period this story was reported, tried to move toward clearer rules. The government’s response was caution. Caution, in this area, usually means the current habit continues.`,
      `This is not only a British problem and not only a drill problem. American courts have admitted rap lyrics for decades, and the criticism has been public for decades: the form is read literally when other violent fictions, from country songs to horror films, are read as fiction. The difference is race and class and the usefulness of the lyrics to a prosecution that wants a narrative. Instrumental hip-hop’s popularity has many causes. One of them, for some artists, is the experience of language being used against the speaker. That is a bad reason for a culture to lose its words. It is an intelligible one.`,
      `A music magazine’s job here is not to pick a verdict in a case it did not sit. It is to refuse the collapse of a verse into a statement. 252 defendants in three years is the scale of the collapse. The Met’s September 2026 defense is the institution saying the collapse is a method. Art Not Evidence is the counter-institution. Between them is a jury, a parent, and a writer who may have been performing. The performance is not a get-out-of-jail card. It is a fact about how the art works. Courts that cannot be bothered to learn the fact will keep trying songs as if they were statements to the police. They are not statements to the police. They are songs, including when they are dangerous.`
    ]
  },
  "hello-kitty-pants": {
    format: "Essay",
    kicker: "Essay",
    dek: "Nine signatures on a 6 February 2026 petition tried to police pajama pants. The flyers were already printing the cat.",
    paras: [
      `On 6 February 2026 a Change.org petition opened by Samantha Scott argued that Hello Kitty pajama pants were ruining raves. The text, as it circulated, called the pants mass-produced sleepwear, “not self-expression” but laziness, and claimed they announced that the wearer had got dressed in the dark. EDM.com counted the signatures. There were nine. By the time this desk wrote about it, in September 2026, the petition had sat untouched by anything resembling a scene: no door policy anyone could point to, no promoter’s statement, a brief life as a joke, and then the ordinary fate of a moral panic that arrived before the outfit did.`,
      `Dress codes at dances are older than raves and usually tell you who is allowed to be comfortable. The stated reasons are safety, respect, a minimum of effort. The operational effect is a filter on class, gender, and body. Rave culture’s own memory of dress is not a tuxedo memory. It is furry boots, kandi, a pacifier worn as a joke that stopped being a joke, sportswear, a flag tied around a waist, whatever would survive heat and a bus. Hello Kitty pajamas fit that history more neatly than the petition does. They are soft, they are a cartoon, they are cheap, and they make an adult look like they have declined the assignment to be sexy in the approved way. The decline is the offense. “Raves are not sleepovers” is the sentence of someone who needs the room to be a stage.`,
      `The interesting counter-evidence was already on paper. A Glasgow night billed around the cat — hardcore, hardstyle, techno — told people to wear the pajamas and promised they would not be judged. One petition wanted the pants gone. A flyer for an actual event wanted them on. Flyers are a better poll than a petition no one signed, because a flyer has a date and a risk. Someone is paying for the room. If they print the cat, the culture has voted with its only real ballot, which is the booking. Nine names on a website are a comments section that found a form.`,
      `None of this requires a defense of every outfit, and the defense-of-the-outfit essay is its own cliché. People wear annoying things. They wear sponsored things. They wear nothing, and that has its own politics. The question worth a magazine’s time is who appointed the emergency. A scene that is genuinely arguing about police, rent, payouts, and whether the music is any good does not need a pajama crisis. The crisis appeared because the visual internet needs a new door to stand at, and a cartoon on a soft fabric is legible at phone size. Legibility is the requirement of the feed, not of the dance. On the dance, you cannot read someone’s trousers in enough detail to build a theory. You can see that they are dancing.`,
      `Comfort as a kind of armor is not new. The baggy shirt, the shoe you can actually stand in, the layer you can lose when the room heats up: these are technical decisions about a long night. A petition that calls them laziness is confused about the task. The task is eight hours on your feet in a loud room. The people who did that task in 1992 and the people who do it now are allowed to solve it differently. Somebody’s older cousin is always available to explain that it used to look like effort. Effort, in the cousin’s memory, was a specific consumer choice that has since been photographed. The photograph is not a rule.`,
      `Scott’s petition will not be the reason anyone is turned away at a door. It is already a document of a mismatch: the internet’s appetite for a dress-code fight, and a dance culture that answered, where it answered at all, by printing the offending cat on the flyer. Nine signatures. A Glasgow booking. Pants that a person can sleep in and dance in, which is a practical virtue the petition managed to frame as a decline. The decline is elsewhere. It is in the idea that a scene needs a uniform in order to be serious. The music is the serious part. The trousers are cloth.`
    ]
  }
};
